// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row struct_pattern_name_check_skipped_under_ref (tier 2). A struct
// pattern naming a DIFFERENT struct than the scrutinee — `match &p { Q { x } => … }`
// over a `&P` — is a type error in Rust and TODAY it compiles rc 0 and RUNS, reading
// P's first field under Q's name. The check is written (sema_stmt.cpp, PAT_STRUCT door,
// "struct pattern: '{}' != scrutinee '{}'") but it is guarded on
// `TypeRef(scrut_type).kind() == LogosType::Kind::Struct`, and a REFERENCE scrutinee is
// not that kind, so the whole comparison is skipped — the guard is a permissive exit,
// not a check. The by-value twin below IS refused, which is the shape of the defect.
// ⚠ NOT closed by 2026-09-05b's peel: the door's chain-collapse leaves `&P`, still not
// Kind::Struct. The unknown-NAME half of the same door does fire under a ref (pinned by
// fail/patpeel_struct_two_ref_layers_unknown.logos) — it is only the MISMATCH half that
// is unreachable behind a reference. Found by the pattern lattice, hand program c30.
// Rust: refused ("mismatched types").
package struct_pattern_name_check_skipped_under_ref;
struct P { x: i64 }
struct Q { x: i64 }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let p: P = P { x: 3i64 };
    match &p {
        Q { x } => { if *x != 3i64 { return 1i32; } }
    }
    return 0i32;
}
